[6c] IS (PIVOT-MAJ) STRICTLY STRONGER THAN (RH)?  -- i.e. is the reduction real work?
     The implication used is STANDARD: for partitions of the same integer,
     a >= b in dominance  ==>  a* <= b* (conjugates)  ==>  #parts(a) = a*_1 <= b*_1 =
     #parts(b).  So (PIVOT-MAJ) ==> (RH).  The converse is NOT automatic; measured:
     partition pairs (a,b) of the same N in [2,12] examined: 12646
     dominance ==> fewer-or-equal parts : holds 5762, FAILS 0
     fewer-or-equal parts WITHOUT dominance: 1458  (so the converse is false; example ((2, 2), (3, 1)))
   CONTROL: the standard implication was actually exercised           hits=5762     OK
   CORRUPT: the CONVERSE implication is violated (so (PIVOT-MAJ) is strictly stronger) hits=1458     OK

